These pages cover the decisions that are cheap to make before a field season and expensive to undo after it: agreeing on one setup, recording context that stands on its own, and verifying backups and transfers.

{% aside type="note" title="Work in progress" %}
These best practices are a living guide and a work in progress. We update and expand them as we learn more from field teams, curators, and NAHPU users, and the challenges they encounter when using NAHPU.
{% /aside %}

{% bpdiagram kind="lifecycle" locale="en" /%}

## How to read these pages

- **Guidance, not rules.** Depart from any recommendation that does not fit your taxa, terrain, or team — just record why so the decision stays legible later.
- **Local protocols come first.** Your institution, museum, funder, permit, or community agreement always governs; use these pages to fill the gaps they leave.
- **Evidence sits in separate notes.** Each page leads with the practical recommendation. Published studies and figures sit in `Note` callouts you can skip or inspect as needed; plain statements of NAHPU behavior carry no citation.

New to NAHPU? Start with [Day One](/en/day-one/) in a practice project, use the [Usage guides](/en/usages/) for fields and controls, and check the [Cookbook](/en/how-to-recipes/) for step-by-step tasks.
